      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "I must first complete the square for the equation.\n\n\\begin{align}\nx^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x^2 - 6x + 9 - 9) + (y^2 + 2y + 1 - 1) + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 - 10 &= 0 \\\\\n(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 &= 10\n\\end{align}\n\nI know this is a circle of radius \\(\\sqrt{10}\\), but how did they come up with the interpretation?\n\nThey have taken the radius of a circle to be the square root of minus the \"number \" on the right hand side of the equation. Put simply, they found the \"number\" to get the radius and I got $\\sqrt{10}$\n\nThe original equation can be solved by completing the square for $x$ and $y$.\nCompleting the square for $x$ and $y$ leaves you with:\n$(x - 3)^2 + (y + 1)^2 = 10$.\nThe center of this is at $(3, -1)$ and since it is equal to 10, you know the radius is \\(\\boxed{\\sqrt{10}}\\).",
