    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "\\begin{align*}\nx^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 &= 0 \\\\\n\\left(x^2 - 6x + 9 \\right) + \\left(y^2 + 2y + 1 \\right) &= -6 + 9 + 1 \\\\\n\\end{align*}\n$\\textbf{(A) }1 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) }2 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) }3 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) }6 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) }9$\n\nWe can use similar statements as in the previous problem to clearly solve the problem.\n$x^2 - 6x + y^2 + 2y + 6 = 0$ becomes $(x-3)^2 - 9 + (y+1)^2 - 1 = -6 + 9 + 1$ by completing the square.\nThis simplifies to $(x-3)^2 + (y+1)^2 = 5$. So we know that (3,-1) is the center, and its radius is $\\sqrt{5}$ (Just take the square root of the right hand side; you're looking for just the radius). Therefore, $2^2 = 5$, which is the case if $(x,y)$ equals $(2,-1)$. $\\textbf{(B) }2$ is the answer.",
